                <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
                <p class="doc-meta">Effective 19 September 2026 &middot; SACC Software Limited</p>
            </header>

            <p class="doc-lede">
                Q-Mgr is front-office software built by SACC Software Limited. Organizations &mdash; businesses,
                hospitals, government offices and schools &mdash; use it to run their queues, front desks, visitor
                logs, communication and, in schools, student welfare and staff records. It therefore handles two
                distinct kinds of personal data, held under two different responsibilities. This policy sets out
                both, and states which one applies to you.
            </p>

            <section class="doc-callout">
                <strong>Who answers for your data depends on which of the two you are.</strong> If you are a
                member of staff at an organization using Q-Mgr, we hold your account data and we answer for it;
                Section 7 sets out how. If you took a queue ticket, checked in at a front desk, received a
                message, opened a shared document, or are a student or guardian at a school using Q-Mgr, that
                organization collected your data and we hold it on their instruction. A correction or a deletion
                therefore starts with them, not with us. Section 8 sets out what to do.
            </section>

            <h2>1. Who this policy covers</h2>
            <p>
                Two groups of people interact with data that passes through Q-Mgr:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Our customers</strong> &mdash; the organizations that hold a Q-Mgr account, and the
                    staff they invite (administrators, managers, counter agents, teachers). We are directly
                    responsible for their account and billing data.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>The people they serve</strong> &mdash; people who take a queue ticket, are checked in at
                    a front desk, receive a Broadcast, give feedback, or open a document shared with them; and, in
                    schools, students and their guardians. In law the organization is the data controller here and
                    we are the data processor: they determine what is collected and why, and we provide the
                    software that stores and moves it.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2>2. What we collect and why</h2>
            <p><strong>From organizations and their staff:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Account details &mdash; name, work email, phone number, role, and a password, stored as a salted hash and never in plain text.</li>
                <li>Organization details &mdash; name, industry, branch locations, timezone, and billing contact.</li>
                <li>Billing information &mdash; the invoices and payments for the modules you use, and the Mobile Money number your renewals are charged to. Payments are collected by the SACC payment gateway (sacc.ug): Mobile Money is approved on the paying phone with its own PIN, and card details are entered on the gateway's own secure page. We never see or store a card number. Phone numbers in our payment logs are stored masked.</li>
                <li>Staff records, in schools that use Welfare &amp; Performance &mdash; employment details the school keeps (such as start date, qualification and registration number), duty and lesson registers, recognition, appraisals, and the records the school logs about a member of staff. Every member of staff can read their own file and reply to what is recorded about them from their own workspace.</li>
                <li>An activity log &mdash; who viewed, changed or exported a record, when, and from which shortened network address and browser. It is how an organization answers the question "who looked at this?". The name of who did something is removed after the organization's retention window, which is one year by default; the line itself remains.</li>
                <li>Usage data &mdash; which features are used, API calls made through your integration keys, and error logs. We use these to keep the service running and to correct faults.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>From the people your organization serves, where these features are used:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Queue tickets</em> &mdash; a name and phone number where the organization asks for them at check-in, together with which counter served the person and how long they waited.</li>
                <li><em>Visitor Management</em> &mdash; full name, phone, email, a national ID or passport number where the site requires it, a check-in photograph where the organization's kiosk captures one, vehicle plate, host name, and purpose of visit. Where a person is placed on a site's watchlist, the reason given for that flag is stored with it. This is among the most sensitive information we hold. It exists so that front-desk staff can be warned, and we do nothing with it beyond storing it and returning it to the organization that entered it.</li>
                <li><em>Broadcasts</em> &mdash; contact details (email, phone, or a Telegram chat ID) that an organization uploads or collects, used to send messages that organization composes. Every message we send on their behalf carries a working unsubscribe link. That is built into the sending pipeline and cannot be switched off.</li>
                <li><em>Feedback</em> &mdash; the answers a person gives to an organization's survey, and a contact detail only where they choose to leave one.</li>
                <li><em>Shared documents</em> &mdash; when a person opens a document an organization has shared with them, we record that it was opened, when, the email address they verified where the link requires one, and a shortened network address and browser. The organization sees this so that it knows who read what it shared. These details are blanked after the organization's chosen retention period.</li>
                <li><em>Students and guardians</em> &mdash; in schools using Welfare &amp; Performance: a student's name, class, date of birth, photograph, guardians and their contact details, and the welfare records the school keeps. Welfare records can be highly sensitive. The school determines who may see them: each record carries a visibility level, class teachers see only their own classes, the most restricted records are visible only to the school's senior administrators, and every export of a child's record is logged. Photographs and evidence files are never served to anyone who may not see the record they belong to.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>3. How long we keep it</h2>
            <p>
                Visitor records expire automatically. We hold them for 730 days &mdash; two years &mdash; and a
                nightly job then deletes them permanently. An organization may set that window to anywhere between
                30 days and 10 years to match its own compliance obligations; the setting is theirs. A visitor who
                is still checked in when their record would otherwise expire is never purged mid-visit, because the
                clock runs only on completed visits.
            </p>
            <p>
                The welfare ledger and staff records are append-only. A correction is added as a note rather than
                overwriting what was written, because a record that can be quietly altered is of no use to the
                child or the adult it concerns. They are held for as long as the school's account is active, or
                until the school asks us to remove them. Queue history, broadcast send logs and billing records are
                held for as long as the organization's account is active, since they are the basis of its reports
                and accounts.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>When an account stops being paid for, its data is deleted in full.</strong> The timetable is
                published in Section 8a of our <a href="/terms">Terms</a>, and we email the account before each
                step: suspended for 60 days, closed for 30 more, scheduled for deletion for a final 14. That is 104
                days in total, with three opportunities to return. At the end, every record belonging to that
                organization is permanently deleted &mdash; students, staff, visitors, welfare records, documents,
                messages, settings and every uploaded file &mdash; and we verify afterwards that nothing was left
                behind. An organization may ask us to bring that forward, or ask for a copy of its data, at any
                point before the final step.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Backups.</strong> Deleted data can still exist inside encrypted database backups until those
                backups expire on their normal schedule, which is 30 days. During that window it is used for nothing
                and is available to no one, and if a backup is restored the deletion is re-applied automatically so
                that nothing returns. After 30 days no copy of it remains anywhere.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Two things outlive a deletion, and neither identifies anybody.</strong> Where an organization
                has paid us, Uganda's Tax Procedures Code requires the tax record of that payment to be kept for five
                years. We keep the invoice and the payment &mdash; amount, date, reference &mdash; and remove the
                contact details attached to them. An organization that never paid has no such record, and nothing is
                kept. We also keep a short marker that an account was deleted and when. It contains no name, no
                address and no email address, only one-way codes that cannot be turned back into a person. It exists
                so that a deleted account cannot quietly reappear, and so that we can answer accurately if someone
                later asks what became of their data.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Who else receives it</h2>
            <p>We do not sell personal data. A small number of processors handle parts of the service on our behalf:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>SACC payment gateway (sacc.ug)</strong> &mdash; collects subscription payments by Mobile Money and card. It receives the paying phone number, or the card payer's name and email, and the amount. It receives nothing else about your organization's data.</li>
                <li><strong>SMS, email, WhatsApp and Telegram gateways</strong> &mdash; used only for organizations that send Broadcasts or notifications, and only to deliver the specific message that was sent.</li>
                <li><strong>Our hosting and database provider</strong> &mdash; where everything above physically resides.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We disclose data to law enforcement only where legally compelled to do so by a valid court order or
                its equivalent, and we inform the affected organization unless we are legally barred from doing so.
            </p>

            <h2>5. How we protect it</h2>
            <p>
                Every connection to Q-Mgr runs over HTTPS. Passwords are hashed and are never stored as plain text.
                Each organization's data is logically separated from every other organization's, and that separation
                is enforced at the database query level rather than in the interface alone, so that a fault in one
                tenant's view cannot expose another tenant's records. Within an organization, access follows the
                roles that organization sets, down to which classes a teacher may see. Uploaded files such as
                photographs and evidence are authorised on every request against the person asking for them. Internal
                access to production data is limited to the people who require it to operate the service.
            </p>
            <p>
                No system is impregnable. If a breach exposes your data, we will inform you promptly and state what
                occurred.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Cookies and local storage</h2>
            <p>
                We use your browser's local storage to keep you signed in between visits and to remember small
                preferences such as your last selected branch, a date range, or how you prefer to read a document.
                Q-Mgr runs no third-party advertising trackers and we sell no browsing data. There is no advertising
                network involved in the product.
            </p>

            <h2>7. If you are a member of staff at an organization using Q-Mgr</h2>
            <p>
                You may review and correct your own account and contact details from your profile or workspace at any
                time, and read and reply to what your school records about you there. To delete your account, ask your
                organization's administrator, or write to
                <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>8. If you are a customer, visitor, student or guardian</h2>
            <p>
                Where an organization checked you into a queue, logged you as a visitor, sent you a message, shared a
                document with you, or keeps records about you or your child as a school, that organization decided to
                collect your data and is the correct first point of contact. It can view, correct or delete that data
                directly. If you cannot reach them, or you would prefer to approach us first, write to
                <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a> with the organization's name and what you
                would like done. We will direct your request to them and, where we are able to act directly, act on it.
            </p>

            <h2>9. Children</h2>
            <p>
                Q-Mgr accounts are for organizations and their adult staff, and we do not knowingly create an account
                for anyone under 18. Schools using Welfare &amp; Performance do keep records about their students in
                Q-Mgr, and the school is responsible for those records as the data controller. We process that data
                only on the school's instruction and to operate the service, and we treat it as the most sensitive
                data we hold. A visitor or queue entry for a minor &mdash; a patient at a clinic, for example &mdash;
                is handled as any other record, under the organization's own responsibility.
            </p>

            <h2>10. Changes to this policy</h2>
            <p>
                Where we make a material change to how we handle personal data, we update the effective date at the
                top of this page. Where a change meaningfully affects existing customers, we also email the account
                administrators directly.
            </p>

            <h2>11. Contact</h2>
            <p>
                Questions, requests or concerns about any of the above:
                <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a>.
            </p>

